{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "A p-polarized HeNe beam passes an input polarizer (0 deg), a 20 mm TGG Faraday rotator and an analyser at -45 deg before a power meter. TGG has a negative Verdet constant (-134 rad/(T m)), so a positive field rotates the polarization towards negative angles. The rotator field is not fixed: an actuator link sets bField = 0.75 T/A x coil current (the gain stands in for an iron-yoke electromagnet). In the circuit a function generator sweeps the coil current between 0 and ~0.4 A at 2 Hz; the rotation theta = V B L swings between 0 and 45 deg, so the transmitted power follows Malus's law between 50 % (no current) and ~95 % (at 0.4 A, polarization aligned with the analyser); at that peak field (0.3 T) the polarizer pair acts as an isolator: a back-reflected beam would be rotated a further 45 deg and blocked by the input polarizer.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Faraday Rotator Modulator"
  },
